    </figure>

    <div class="prose-tca">
      <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", a Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. I analyzed the Aeron Size C thoroughly while researching chairs before purchasing the Steelcase Gesture. This review draws on Herman Miller's published specifications, biomechanical principles from my engineering coursework, and aggregated feedback from tall users across r/ergonomics, r/officechair, and r/tallpeople. I haven't personally sat in the Aeron — my analysis is spec-driven and community-informed, not first-hand.</p>
        <p>The Herman Miller Aeron Size C is the largest variant in Herman Miller's iconic Aeron line, specifically engineered for users 6'0" and taller. Since its original introduction in 1994 and remaster in 2016, the Aeron has established itself as a benchmark in ergonomic seating. The PostureFit SL lumbar system and Pellicle mesh suspension are genuinely differentiated technologies — not marketing copy.</p>
        <p>For tall users, the Size C addresses real dimensional gaps that standard chairs ignore: seat depth, back height, and overall frame scale. The question for any given tall person isn't whether the Aeron is good — it is — but whether the fixed 18.5" seat depth matches their specific femur length. That single spec drives most of the fit decisions in this category.</p>
      </section>

      <CompanionPicks
        intro={"Size C fixes the frame size. At 6'2\" and above the back still ends below the base of the skull, and the Aeron is the only chair covered here with a healthy aftermarket headrest supply."}
        picks={[
          {
            href: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0B86SYST5?tag=tcaaccessory-20",
